// Formative practice quiz for lifting-state-up. Low-stakes; answers are visible by design. First draft, not yet reviewed.
$CATEGORY: react/effects-routing-and-rendering/lifting-state-up

::lifting-state-up o1 - closest common parent::Two sibling components must show the same selected value. Where should the state live?{
~In both siblings, kept the same by hand#Not quite. Two copies can disagree.
~In the first sibling only#Not quite. A sibling cannot read another sibling's state.
~As high up as possible, always at the root#Not quite. Keep it as low as still works, and not higher than needed.
=In their closest common parent#Correct. That parent can pass the value down to both.
}

::lifting-state-up o2 - child asks parent::How does a child change state that its parent owns?{
=It calls an event handler prop that the parent passed down#Correct. The parent keeps ownership, and the child just asks.
~It assigns to the prop directly#Not quite. Props are read-only to the child.
~It imports the parent state setter from the parent file#Not quite. A state setter exists only inside a render, and it cannot be imported.
~It calls useState with the same name#Not quite. That creates separate state in the child.
}

::lifting-state-up o2 - handler prop naming::A Row gets a prop called onSelect. What is the correct way to use it on a button?{
~onClick set to a call of onSelect with parentheses#Not quite. That calls it during render, not on click.
~Calling onSelect at the top of Row#Not quite. That runs on every render.
=onClick set to onSelect, passing the function itself#Correct. React calls the function on click.
~Setting onSelect to a new value inside Row#Not quite. Props are read-only to the child.
}

::lifting-state-up o3 - derived value::A component stores firstName and lastName, and also stores fullName in state. What is the better design?{
~Keep it and update all three together everywhere#Not quite. Any missed update makes them disagree.
=Compute fullName during render from the two names#Correct. A computed value cannot get out of step.
~Add an Effect to copy the names into fullName#Not quite. That is more code and an extra render, for a simple calculation.
~Remove firstName and lastName, and parse fullName#Not quite. Parsing a joined string is fragile, and you lose the source data.
}
